From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 01:16:11 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] scsi: ufs: exynos: Tell the PHY about hibern8 before stopping
 its clocks

Once the link is in hibern8 the host stops its clocks and then hands the
PHY its table for that state.  The clocks it stops include the APB clock
of the PHY, which is the one those register writes go through, so the
table is written with that clock already stopped.

Write the table first and stop the clocks after it, which is the order
the vendor kernel for these SoCs uses.  Only SoCs whose PHY has hibern8
tables are affected; for the others the notification does nothing.

Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/ufs/host/ufs-exynos.c | 9 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-exynos.c b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-exynos.c
index 07a8a8d5232..135454a8e27 100644
--- a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-exynos.c
+++ b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-exynos.c
@@ -1721,11 +1721,16 @@ static void exynos_ufs_post_hibern8(struct ufs_hba *hba, enum uic_cmd_dme cmd)
 
 	if (cmd == UIC_CMD_DME_HIBER_ENTER) {
 		ufs->entry_hibern8_t = ktime_get();
+
+		/*
+		 * The PHY is written through its APB clock, which the clock
+		 * gating below stops as well, so tell the PHY first.
+		 */
+		phy_notify_state(ufs->phy, phystate);
+
 		exynos_ufs_gate_clks(ufs);
 		if (ufs->opts & EXYNOS_UFS_OPT_BROKEN_AUTO_CLK_CTRL)
 			exynos_ufs_enable_auto_ctrl_hcc(ufs);
-
-		phy_notify_state(ufs->phy, phystate);
 	}
 }
 
